.SH DESCRIPTION
The _printf function is a lightweight, custom version of the standard C printf. It takes a format string, looks through it character by character, and prints everything to standard output. Regular characters get written directly, but whenever it runs into a %, it knows a special instruction is coming next.

After the %, the next character tells _printf what kind of value it should print and which argument to pull from the variadic list. Depending on the specifier, it calls helper functions to handle the actual printing.


The following conversion specifiers are supported:

.TP
.B %d, %i
Print a signed decimal integer.

.TP
.B %c
Print a single character.

.TP
.B %s
Print a null-terminated string.

.TP
.B %%
Print a literal percent sign.

If the format string is NULL, or if a percent sign appears at the end of the 
format string without a valid specifier, the function returns -1. Otherwise, 
it returns the total number of characters printed.





.SH EXAMPLES
1. Printing a string:
.br
.B _printf("Hello, %s!\\n", "Everyone!");
.br
Output:
.br
Hello, Everyone!

.PP
2. Printing an integer:
.br
.B _printf("Score: %d\\n", 98);
.br
Output:
.br
Score: 98

.PP
3. Printing a character:
.br
.B _printf("Grade: %c\\n", 'A');
.br
Output:
.br
Grade: A

.PP
4. Printing a percent sign:
.br
.B _printf("Completion: 100%%\\n");
.br
Output:
.br
Completion: 100%

.PP
5. Mixing different types:
.br
.B _printf("Name: %s, Age: %d, Initial: %c\\n", "Reem", 21, 'R');
.br
Output:
.br
Name: Reem, Age: 21, Initial: R

